**Design issue:** The caller learns the server is ready through **busy-waiting (polling)** — `wait_ready` spins in a tight loop, sleeping 50 ms between checks. This wastes CPU cycles and introduces up to 50 ms of latency even when the server becomes ready immediately.

**Replacement pattern:** **`std::condition_variable`** — the background worker sets `ready = true` and calls `notify_one()`, while the caller blocks on `wait()` with a predicate. The caller wakes up *immediately* when notified, with no polling overhead and no unnecessary latency.

A corrected version would look like:

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>

class Server {
public:
  std::atomic<bool> ready{false};
  std::mutex mtx;
  std::condition_variable cv;
  // background worker: ready = true; cv.notify_one();
};

void wait_ready(Server& s) {
  std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(s.mtx);
  s.cv.wait(lk, [&s]{ return s.ready.load(); });
}
```

